# What NOT to save in git.
#
# Anything matching a line here is invisible to git. That is what you want
# for things that are large, machine-specific, or can be regenerated -- and
# especially for anything secret.
#
# To check whether a file is ignored:  git check-ignore -v <file>

# ----- Robot pulls -----
# Raw copies of everything on the Control Hub. Tens of megabytes, and
# regenerated any time with scripts/pull-from-robot.sh.
hub-snapshots/

# ----- Build output -----
# Produced by scripts/check-code.sh and by Java tools. Nothing here is
# source; it is all rebuilt from teamcode/.
tools/out/
*.class
bin/
out/
build/

# ----- Secrets -----
# NEVER commit passwords, Wi-Fi keys or tokens. Git keeps history forever,
# so deleting a password in a later commit does NOT remove it -- the only
# real fix is changing the password. If it happens, say so straight away.
.env
*.key
*.pem
secrets.*
**/credentials*

# ----- Editor and operating system clutter -----
# Specific to one person's machine, so it only causes conflicts.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/
*.iml
*.swp
*~
.#*

# ----- Scratch files -----
# Convention: anything with "scratch" or "tmp" in the name is yours alone.
# Use it for experiments you do not want to share.
scratch/
tmp/
*.tmp
*.bak
*.orig

# ----- Deliberately NOT ignored -----
# These look like build output but are kept on purpose:
#
#   tools/lib/*.jar          the robot's own FTC libraries, so code can be
#                            checked on a laptop with no internet
#   history/onbot-builds/*.zip  the hub's backup of every build, which is how
#                            an overwritten version was recovered
#   logs/*.csv               diagnostic logs; a log showing a real failure is
#                            worth keeping next to the fix
